Verify CTK component dependencies instead of assuming them

MITK modules can declare a dependency on specific COMPONENTS of a package
using the "Package|Component" syntax, e.g. Modules/XNAT:

    MITK_CREATE_MODULE(
      DEPENDS MitkCore MitkQtWidgets
      PACKAGE_DEPENDS
        PUBLIC CTK|CTKXNATCore
        PRIVATE Qt6|UiTools+Xml+Widgets
    )

mitkFunctionCheckModuleDependencies parses those component lists but never
checks them: the only test applied is whether MITK_USE_<Package> is enabled.
A module is therefore enabled whenever the *package* was found, even if the
specific component it needs was never built.

MITK_CTK_Config.cmake then does

    list(APPEND ALL_INCLUDE_DIRECTORIES ${${_ctk_component}_INCLUDE_DIRS})
    list(APPEND ALL_LIBRARIES ${_ctk_component})

with an undefined ${_ctk_component}_INCLUDE_DIRS, so the module configures
cleanly with an empty include path and the failure is deferred all the way to
compile time, as a pile of "no such file or directory" on the component's
headers.

Concretely: a CTK built without CTK_LIB_XNAT/Core (the upstream default is OFF,
and it cannot currently be turned on for Qt6 because Libs/XNAT/Core/ctkXnatAPI.cpp
still uses QScriptEngine/QScriptValue from the removed Qt5 QtScript module)
still enables MitkXNAT, which then fails on the 20 ctkXnat*.h headers that do
not exist.

Verify the component targets during the dependency check so the module is
disabled the same way any other unmet dependency is.

The test is restricted to CTK deliberately. CTK exports every component under
its own name (CTKCore, CTKXNATCore, ...), so "is there a target with this name"
is exact. Other packages do not follow that convention -- Qt6's components are
Widgets/Xml/UiTools while its targets are Qt6::Widgets etc. -- and applying the
same test there disables essentially every module, MitkCore included.

Verified against MITK 2026.06 with a CTK lacking XNAT: MitkXNAT moves from the
enabled to the disabled feature list, is no longer generated as a build target,
and nothing else changes (CTK plugins 29 -> 29, enabled features 145 -> 144,
ninja edges 7352 -> 7318, i.e. only XNAT's own).

--- a/CMake/mitkFunctionCheckModuleDependencies.cmake
+++ b/CMake/mitkFunctionCheckModuleDependencies.cmake
@@ -50,6 +50,23 @@
     foreach(_package ${package_names})
       if((DEFINED MITK_USE_${_package}) AND NOT (${MITK_USE_${_package}}))
         list(APPEND missing_deps ${_package})
+      elseif(_package STREQUAL "CTK")
+        # A module may require specific COMPONENTS of a package (the
+        # "CTK|CTKXNATCore" syntax). MITK parses those but never verifies them,
+        # so the module is enabled whenever the *package* is found even if the
+        # component was never built -- MITK_CTK_Config.cmake then appends an
+        # empty ${component}_INCLUDE_DIRS and the failure is deferred to compile
+        # time. CTK exports each component under its own name, so a plain TARGET
+        # test is exact here. Restricted to CTK on purpose: other packages (Qt6)
+        # use component names that are not bare target names (Widgets ->
+        # Qt6::Widgets), where this test would wrongly disable everything.
+        foreach(_vis PUBLIC PRIVATE INTERFACE)
+          foreach(_component ${${_vis}_${_package}_REQUIRED_COMPONENTS})
+            if(NOT TARGET ${_component})
+              list(APPEND missing_deps "${_package}|${_component}")
+            endif()
+          endforeach()
+        endforeach()
       endif()
     endforeach()
   endif()
